@database "audio_ops"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: audio_ops.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Audio operations"
@{h1}Audio operations

Lets an agent edit sound files: cut a piece out or remove it, join files
one after another, lay one over another, make a file louder or quieter,
find and shorten pauses, and make a file of silence. It can also play a
file, or a piece of it, to its model, so a model that takes audio input
can listen to what it made. The plugin works in one storage folder; every
file name an agent gives is taken inside it. FLAC, MP3 and WAV are
supported.

The plugin has no panel and no hooks. It also brings a command-line tool,
@{tt}audio-ops@{utt}.

@{code}
For agents and developers
  @{"  Looking at files: info, list, load  " link look}
  @{"  Editing: cut, merge, mix, volume, create  " link edit}
  @{"  Pauses: detect_silence, compress_silence  " link silence}
  @{"  The storage folder and its paths  " link storage}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Limits  " link limits}
  @{"  The audio-ops command line  " link cli}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node look "Looking at files: info, list, load"
@{h1}Looking at files

The tool names are the instance name followed by the operation:
@{tt}audio_ops_info@{utt}, @{tt}audio_ops_list@{utt},
@{tt}audio_ops_load@{utt}. File names count inside the storage folder
(@{" The storage folder and its paths " link storage}).

@{h2}info

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
file | the file to inspect
@{body}

The file is decoded in full. The answer: @{tt}file@{utt},
@{tt}duration_seconds@{utt} (two places), @{tt}format@{utt} (from the
extension), @{tt}channels@{utt}, @{tt}sample_rate@{utt},
@{tt}sample_width_bytes@{utt} and @{tt}size_bytes@{utt}.

@{h2}list

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
pattern | a glob inside the storage folder, e.g. *.wav or voices/*.flac; without it, the .flac, .mp3 and .wav files directly in the folder
@{body}

Only files with one of the three extensions are listed, sorted by path.
Each is decoded in full to measure its length, so a folder of long files
takes a while. An entry has @{tt}name@{utt}, @{tt}size_bytes@{utt},
@{tt}duration_seconds@{utt} and @{tt}format@{utt}, or @{tt}error@{utt}
("Cannot read: ...") instead of the last two when it cannot be decoded.
The answer adds @{tt}storage_path@{utt} and @{tt}total_count@{utt}. A
pattern with a @{tt}..@{utt} part is refused (@{tt}SecurityError@{utt}),
an absolute one ends in "Non-relative patterns are unsupported".

@{h2}load

Attaches the file, or a piece of it, to the answer, for the model to
listen to.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
file | the file to play
start_time | seconds; with end_time or alone, loads only a piece
end_time | seconds; beyond the end it is cut to the file's length
@{body}

Without a time the file itself is attached. With one, the piece is
written as a WAV file into the storage folder
(@{tt}_temp_segment_<name>_<start ms>_<end ms>_<random>.wav@{utt}) and
that is attached. A negative start or an end not after the start is a
@{tt}ValidationError@{utt}, a start past the end a
@{tt}TimeRangeError@{utt}.

A file longer than @{tt}max_duration_seconds@{utt} is refused, also
for a piece of it (@{" Limits " link limits}).

What is attached may be at most 25 MB -- the limit of the request that
carries it. A larger file or piece is refused as @{tt}FileTooLarge@{utt}
("Load a shorter segment."), and a piece that was already written is
deleted again. As WAV, CD-quality stereo reaches 25 MB after about two and
a half minutes; 24 kHz mono after about nine.

The answer: @{tt}file@{utt}, @{tt}duration_seconds@{utt} (of the whole
file), @{tt}format@{utt}, @{tt}mime_type@{utt}, and for a piece
@{tt}segment@{utt} with @{tt}start_time@{utt}, @{tt}end_time@{utt} and
@{tt}segment_duration_seconds@{utt}.

@{code json}
{"file": "chapter_03.flac", "start_time": 60, "end_time": 90}
@{body}
@endnode

@node edit "Editing: cut, merge, mix, volume, create"
@{h1}Editing

Every tool here reads whole files into memory, works on them there and
writes @{tt}dest_file@{utt}, whose extension (.flac, .mp3 or .wav) picks
the format; anything else is a @{tt}FormatError@{utt}. An existing
destination is overwritten without asking, and it may be the source
itself. Missing folders on the way to it are created.

Three limits apply (@{" Limits " link limits}): an input above
@{tt}max_input_mb@{utt} is refused before it is decoded; an input or a
result longer than @{tt}max_duration_seconds@{utt} is refused
(@{tt}TooLong@{utt}, nothing written) -- so a piece cannot be cut out of
a longer file either; and ffmpeg gets @{tt}ffmpeg_timeout_seconds@{utt}.

The destination is replaced only by a finished file: the result is
written as WAV to a temp file next to it (@{tt}.audio_ops_...@{utt}),
encoded by ffmpeg into a second one for MP3 (192 kbit/s) or FLAC, and
then moved into place. A failed or timed-out encode leaves the old file
as it was and removes both temp files.

@{h2}cut

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
source_file | the file to cut
dest_file | the result
start_time, end_time | seconds; start at least 0, end after start and not past the file's end
mode | extract (default): keep the piece; remove: keep everything else
@{body}

A start or end past the file's end is a @{tt}TimeRangeError@{utt}; here
the end is not shortened to fit. The answer: @{tt}mode@{utt},
@{tt}source@{utt}, @{tt}destination@{utt}, the two times,
@{tt}source_duration_seconds@{utt}, @{tt}result_duration_seconds@{utt},
@{tt}format@{utt} and @{tt}operation@{utt} ("Extracted 10.0s-30.0s").

@{h2}merge

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
source_files | two or more files, joined in this order
dest_file | the result
crossfade_ms | overlap between neighbours in milliseconds, default 0; never longer than the shorter of the two
@{body}

Files of different rate or channel count are brought to the highest of
them. The answer: @{tt}source_files@{utt}, @{tt}source_count@{utt},
@{tt}destination@{utt}, @{tt}total_source_duration_seconds@{utt},
@{tt}result_duration_seconds@{utt}, @{tt}crossfade_ms@{utt},
@{tt}format@{utt}. A missing file names its index.

@{h2}mix

Lays @{tt}file2@{utt} over @{tt}file1@{utt}. The shorter one is padded
with silence, so the result is as long as the longer. It takes the
highest rate, channel count and sample width of the two.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
file1, file2 | the two files
dest_file | the result
mix_factor | 0.0 only file1, 1.0 only file2, default 0.5
envelope | instead of mix_factor: two or more points {"time": seconds, "factor": 0.0-1.0}
@{body}

A fixed factor turns each file down by its share (0.5 is -6 dB for both)
and adds them. An envelope is sorted by time, drawn as straight lines
between the points and held flat before the first and after the last.
Both together, a factor outside 0 to 1 or an envelope with fewer than two
points is a @{tt}ValidationError@{utt}. The answer adds the two lengths
and either @{tt}mix_factor@{utt} or @{tt}envelope@{utt} and
@{tt}envelope_points@{utt}.

@{code json}
{"file1": "voice.wav", "file2": "music.flac", "dest_file": "scene.flac",
 "envelope": [{"time": 0, "factor": 0.8}, {"time": 3, "factor": 0.2}]}
@{body}

@{h2}volume

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
source_file, dest_file | input and result
gain_db | a fixed change, -60 to +24 dB
envelope | instead of gain_db: two or more points {"time": seconds, "gain_db": -60 to +24}
normalize | true: afterwards raise the loudest peak to 0 dB
@{body}

At least one of the three is needed; gain_db and envelope exclude each
other. The envelope works like mix's. Normalizing only raises: a file
that already peaks at 0 dB stays as it is. Samples beyond full scale are
clipped. The answer: @{tt}source@{utt}, @{tt}destination@{utt},
@{tt}duration_seconds@{utt}, @{tt}format@{utt}, @{tt}normalized@{utt},
and @{tt}gain_db@{utt} or the envelope.

@{h2}create

Writes a file of silence, 16 bit.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
dest_file | the result
duration_ms | length in milliseconds, above 0
sample_rate | 8000, 16000, 22050, 24000, 44100 (default), 48000 or 96000
channels | 1 or 2 (default)
@{body}

A length above @{tt}max_duration_seconds@{utt} is refused
(@{tt}TooLong@{utt}); the silence is built in memory.
@endnode

@node silence "Pauses: detect_silence, compress_silence"
@{h1}Pauses

Both tools run ffmpeg's @{tt}silencedetect@{utt} filter on the file.
ffmpeg and ffprobe must be on the path; any file ffmpeg can read is
taken.

@{h2}detect_silence

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
source_file | the file
threshold_db | quieter than this is silence, default -40
min_duration | shortest pause reported, seconds, default 0.3
@{body}

The answer: @{tt}silence_count@{utt}, @{tt}silences@{utt} (each
@{tt}start@{utt}, @{tt}end@{utt}, @{tt}duration@{utt} in seconds), the
two settings and @{tt}source_file@{utt}. A pause that runs to the end of
the file ends at the length ffprobe measures. If ffmpeg fails, the answer
is a @{tt}ProcessingError@{utt} with its message, not an empty list.

@{h2}compress_silence

Shortens every pause longer than @{tt}max_silence@{utt} to
@{tt}max_silence@{utt}, keeping half of it at each end; pauses are looked
for from 0.3 seconds on.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
source_file, dest_file | input and result (.flac, .mp3 or .wav)
max_silence | seconds, above 0, default 1.0
threshold_db | as above, default -40
mp3_bitrate | kbit/s for an .mp3 result, default 192
@{body}

The result is always encoded anew, also when nothing had to be shortened.
The answer: @{tt}compressed_count@{utt}, @{tt}time_saved@{utt},
@{tt}original_duration@{utt}, @{tt}new_duration@{utt},
@{tt}source_file@{utt}, @{tt}dest_file@{utt}; with nothing shortened only
the count (0), @{tt}time_saved@{utt} (0.0) and the two names.

The numbers go into ffmpeg's filter text, so both tools turn them into
numbers first; anything else is refused
(@{tt}error_type: ValueError@{utt}) before ffmpeg runs.
@endnode

@node storage "The storage folder and its paths"
@{h1}The storage folder and its paths

All files live in @{tt}storage_path@{utt}
(@{" Setting it up " link setup}). A name from the model is read like
this:

@{bullet}Backslashes count as slashes. A leading storage path, as
configured, is dropped, so a path another tool reported
(@{tt}data/audio_ops/take1.wav@{utt}) names the same file; so is a
leading part that repeats the storage path's first folders.
@{bullet}A @{tt}..@{utt} part is refused (@{tt}SecurityError@{utt}); two
dots inside a name (@{tt}take..2.wav@{utt}) are not.
@{bullet}The name is then resolved -- links followed -- and must still lie
inside the storage folder. An absolute path elsewhere, or a link that
points out, is refused: "File must be within storage directory".
@{bullet}A network or device path (@{tt}\\\\host\\share\\x.wav@{utt},
@{tt}//host/share/x.wav@{utt}, @{tt}\\\\?\\UNC\\...@{utt}) is refused on
its text alone, before anything is opened -- resolving it would make
Windows sign in to that host with your credentials.

@{h2}Who sees which files

With a plain @{tt}storage_path@{utt} there is one folder for everyone:
every agent with the tools, in every session and of every user, can list,
play and overwrite every file in it.

With @{tt}{session_id}@{utt} in the path each session gets its own
folder, created on the first write, and cannot reach another's: a path
into another session's folder lands in its own. A sub-agent runs in a
session of its own, so it does not see its caller's files. A call
without a session works in the path with @{tt}{session_id}@{utt} left
out -- the folder that holds all the session folders.

@{h2}What is left behind

The plugin deletes nothing, apart from a piece @{tt}load@{utt} had to
refuse. The @{tt}_temp_segment_...wav@{utt} pieces of @{tt}load@{utt}
stay, because every later request of the run reads them again
(@{" What the model sees " link model}); @{tt}list@{utt} shows them like
any other file. Clearing the folder is up to you.

The writing tools are not recorded by
@{" file checkpoints " link file_checkpoints/recorded}: a rewind counts
them but cannot restore what they overwrote.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

Ten tools, described in the plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}. Every
answer is JSON. A refusal is an answer, not an exception:
@{tt}status: error@{utt}, @{tt}error@{utt}, @{tt}error_type@{utt} and
@{tt}details@{utt}. Texts the model gets include:

@{code}
File must be within storage directory: <name>
Invalid filename: <name>. Path traversal not allowed.
Unsupported audio format: .ogg. Supported: <the three>
end_time (95.0s) exceeds file duration (90.12s)
Audio is too large for the context: 31.4 MB (limit 25 MB). Load a shorter segment.
Cannot specify both mix_factor and envelope. Use one or the other.
ffmpeg silencedetect failed: <ffmpeg's message>
Result would be 5400 s long (limit 3600 s)
Input is longer than 3600 s (limit): <name>
Input file is too large: 312.0 MB (limit 200 MB): <name>
ffmpeg timed out after 300 s
@{body}

The @{tt}error_type@{utt} values: ValidationError, SecurityError,
FormatError, FileNotFoundError, AudioLoadError, DependencyError (pydub
missing), TimeRangeError, FileTooLarge, TooLong, Timeout,
ProcessingError, and in the two pause tools ValueError and
UnexpectedError. The limits are not in the tool descriptions; the model
learns them from the refusal, which names the limit.

The chat's status line ends with what was done: "Created scene.flac
(12.40s) - mixed with factor 0.5", "3 silence segment(s) -- take1.wav",
"Loaded 30.0s segment 60.0s-90.0s of 312.4s, audio/wav -- chapter_03.flac".

@{h2}The attached sound

Only @{tt}load@{utt} attaches anything. The conversation keeps the file's
path; each request to the model reads the file again and sends it along,
so it must stay where it is. Whether the model hears it depends on the
model and its provider, as for
@{" media_ops " link media_ops/model}: a model without audio input gets a
note instead.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Answers are appended; nothing earlier changes. An answer costs tens of
tokens, a list or a pause list more with every entry. A loaded sound costs
by its length -- the core's context estimate counts 41 tokens per second
-- on every later request of the run, since it stays in the conversation.
Loading a piece instead of the whole file is the way to keep that down.
The attachment is built the same way each time, so it does not break the
prompt cache.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}Files are decoded whole into memory, up to
@{tt}max_duration_seconds@{utt}: at the default an hour of CD-quality
stereo, about 600 MB, per input (a mix holds two).
@{bullet}A cancelled call returns at once, but the work in progress runs on
in its thread -- at most until @{tt}ffmpeg_timeout_seconds@{utt} where
ffmpeg does it.
@endnode

@node limits "Limits"
@{h1}Limits

Three server settings (@{" Setting it up " link setup}). A missing value,
or one that is not a positive number, means the default.

@{table}
Setting | Default | What it refuses
max_duration_seconds | 3600 | an input longer than this -- decoding stops there, so a small file of long silence cannot fill the memory; and a result longer than this before it is built: create, merge (checked after each input, crossfades counted), compress_silence
max_input_mb | 200 | an input file larger than this, before it is decoded or handed to ffmpeg -- every tool that reads a file; list shows such a file with an error. It bounds the file, not the memory its decoding takes
ffmpeg_timeout_seconds | 300 | every ffmpeg and ffprobe run that outlasts it; the process is killed and the tool answers Timeout
@{body}

@{h2}Where the length is not checked

@{bullet}@{tt}detect_silence@{utt} and @{tt}compress_silence@{utt} hand the
file to ffmpeg, which streams it: their input may be longer. With nothing
to shorten, @{tt}compress_silence@{utt} copies it at its own length.

@{h2}What the timeout covers

The plugin's own ffmpeg and ffprobe runs (both pause tools and every MP3
or FLAC it writes), and the ffmpeg and ffprobe that pydub starts to read
MP3 and FLAC. pydub's calls are bounded only inside this plugin; other
users of pydub in the same process are not affected. WAV is read and
written without ffmpeg and needs no timeout. ffmpeg starts no processes
of its own, so killing it ends the whole run (measured on Windows).
@endnode

@node cli "The audio-ops command line"
@{h1}The audio-ops command line

@{tt}audio-ops@{utt} does the same ten jobs from a terminal. It is its own
program, not a client of the server: it reads @{tt}storage_path@{utt} of
the @{tt}audio_ops@{utt} entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt} (a
@{tt}{session_id}@{utt} in it is not filled in), or the folder given
with @{tt}-w@{utt}.

@{code}
audio-ops info song.mp3
audio-ops cut input.wav output.wav -s 10 -e 30 -m remove
audio-ops merge output.mp3 part1.mp3 part2.mp3 --crossfade 500
audio-ops mix voice.wav music.wav mixed.wav -f 0.7
audio-ops volume input.wav louder.wav --gain 6 --normalize
audio-ops create silence.wav -d 5000 -r 48000 -c 1
audio-ops detect-silence podcast.flac -t -40 -d 0.5 --json
audio-ops compress-silence input.flac output.mp3 -m 1.5 -b 192
audio-ops list "*.wav"
audio-ops load song.mp3 -s 10 -e 30
audio-ops -w D:/recordings info take1.wav
@{body}

@{tt}audio-ops --help@{utt} and @{tt}audio-ops <command> --help@{utt} list
the options.

It writes files the same way as the tools -- a finished file replaces the
old one, MP3 at 192 kbit/s. Otherwise it is meant for your own files and
differs:

@{bullet}No limits: no @{tt}max_duration_seconds@{utt},
@{tt}max_input_mb@{utt} or ffmpeg timeout; Ctrl+C stops it.
@{bullet}Paths are joined to the folder as given; a stored path prefix is
not stripped, and network paths are not refused on their text.
@{bullet}@{tt}mix@{utt} and @{tt}volume@{utt} take no envelope;
@{tt}detect-silence@{utt} reports pauses from 0.5 seconds on by default.
@{bullet}@{tt}load@{utt} plays nothing: it prints the file's data, and
with @{tt}-s@{utt}/@{tt}-e@{utt} writes the piece to
@{tt}_temp_segment_<name>.wav@{utt}.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    audio_ops:
      type: audio_ops
      enabled: true
      description: "Audio file manipulation - cut segments from FLAC/MP3/WAV files"
      storage_path: "data/audio_ops"
@{body}

An agent sees the tools when its allowlist names them:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+audio_ops/*"
@{body}

Dependencies: pydub and numpy (installed with the plugin) and ffmpeg with
ffprobe on the path. Without ffmpeg, WAV still works for most tools, but
MP3, FLAC and both pause tools do not.

@{h2}Server settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
storage_path | the data directory's audio_ops folder | the folder every file name counts in; {session_id} in it gives each session its own folder beneath
max_duration_seconds | 3600 | longest input and result, see Limits
max_input_mb | 200 | largest input file
ffmpeg_timeout_seconds | 300 | longest ffmpeg or ffprobe run
@{body}

The keys are read from the entry itself (not from a @{tt}config:@{utt}
block) when the server starts. A relative path counts from the folder the
server started in; a @{tt}data/...@{utt} path follows the data directory
when it is moved.

A second instance with its own folder, for example one per session:

@{code yaml}
    session_audio:
      type: audio_ops
      enabled: true
      storage_path: "data/audio/{session_id}"
@{body}
@endnode
